WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.510
Patient and Public Voice
Tim Kelsey

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:12.200
National Director for Patients and Information
Introduction

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:18.340
Absolutely and of course, we heard that incredibly
clearly at the brilliant AGM yesterday.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:23.430
I have to say, I must thank my staff for having
done such a brilliant job in making that happen.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:29.900
It was the first even AGM in NHS history and
what I think we heard really clearly was the

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:35.850
fundamental importance to so many people in
that room of greater participation, of NHS

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:41.160
England really making a substantial, proper
difference in the way in which the NHS properly

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.060
accounts for the patient voice, listens and
learns to listen.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:50.820
That was a phrase that has stayed with me:
one of the feedback panellists described the

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:59.420
important of NHS England and the NHS at large,
'Learning to walk in the shoes of the patient'.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.269
In a sense, that may not necessarily, at first
glance, be the point of this paper, but it

00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:04.619
is the point of this paper.

00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:12.660
This is all about how we can listen properly
to those we are serving.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:20.860
In the paper this time, we focus specifically
on what we are doing in relation to feedback

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:21.860
and voice.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:22.860
Friends and Family Test
Background

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.440
The friends and family test launched in July.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.970
It was unprecedented around the world.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.690
There are health systems which use the friends
and family test -- or, as some people know

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:38.280
it, the 'net promoter score' -- and in this
test, we are simply asking people to tell

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.050
us whether they would recommend a service
to their friends and family, in a way providing

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:47.730
us with a gauge of satisfaction and the quality
of their experience.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:52.950
Thus far, however, the friends and family
test has principally been used by other industries,

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:53.950
to enormous effect.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:02:00.140
So impressive, in fact, are the results from
a variety of industries around the world that

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.820
not only does the friends and family test
provide a proper accountability of quality

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:10.119
in a service but that actually, it drives
real improvement inside the service, that

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:14.000
this is why we have so seriously adopted it
for the NHS.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.260
As I say, we are the first health system which
has adopted it nationally anywhere in the

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.260
world.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:25.870
Response rates
As the paper describes, we have now had two

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:30.420
months of publication; more than half a million
patients have given us their views across

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:37.810
the entirety of NHS England in the context
of in patient wards and A&amp;E departments.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:42.490
Response rates have been much better than
we had anticipated and broadly put, nationally,

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:46.620
the response rate has been over 15% of all
patients in those contexts, which I think

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:47.820
is staggering.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.790
I think it is an amazing achievement.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:54.790
Obviously, in many places, as we have just
heard, there are challenges around response

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:59.819
rates; particularly in A&amp;E departments where,
I think, the pressures of everyday working

00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:04.230
in current circumstances and the way in which
people use A&amp;E departments -- the way they

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.740
flow in and out -- has meant that it has been
more difficult to offer them the opportunity

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.910
of giving feedback on services.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:15.760
In some cases, I am really hoping that publication
of response rates will help drive up, as it

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.590
were, local concentration on different approaches
to making it happen.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:25.020
For example, when one big hospital -- King's,
in London -- started collecting the data,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.030
they were reporting back 3% response rates
in their A&amp;E department.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:29.629
They were very unhappy about that.

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:34.520
They felt that they definitely wanted to get
that higher and with a combination of promotional

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:39.709
activity on site and with the local community,
they now have that well over 15%.

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:43.709
Consequently, I think that there is much which
can be done and we are supporting providers

00:03:43.709 --> 00:03:45.420
who are finding it difficult in A&amp;Es.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.520
In the in patient ward context, however, the
response rates have actually been very, very

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:49.790
high; sometimes they are over 80%.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.460
That, then, is really getting to a point where
it becomes very, very valuable.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.830
Enthusiasm
I have spent quite a lot of time myself testing,

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:01.879
in a way, this presumption that something
which has been so important in other industries

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:05.830
can have real value in healthcare, which is
a very different context than, say, a retailer

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:08.800
or a bank.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.080
I went on a visit to another London hospital
-- the Chelsea and Westminster Hospital -- where,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.510
honestly, there was such a level of enthusiasm
amongst the nursing staff in the A&amp;E department

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:24.539
for a conversation which they had not really
had before, a means of a patient telling them

00:04:24.539 --> 00:04:27.779
very directly that they did not like the environment:
it was too noisy, they felt isolated, they

00:04:27.779 --> 00:04:30.419
did not feel they were being communicated
with.

00:04:30.419 --> 00:04:35.009
It was incredibly empowering for the nurses
and the doctors to be able to get on and change

00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:38.169
the way they were doing things.

00:04:38.169 --> 00:04:42.809
The paper includes some other examples of
ways in which, right now, right at the start,

00:04:42.809 --> 00:04:48.949
this is already -- I think -- driving different
attitudes to listening and to driving improvement.

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:49.990
That is the friends and family test.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:52.300
Methodology
I have one other point to raise, without going

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:53.300
on for too long.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:56.439
There has been quite a lot of discussion about
the methodology of the friends and family

00:04:56.439 --> 00:05:04.780
test and the NHS, like many public services
has -- correctly -- become very used to measuring

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:09.849
itself, where it can, with hard administrative
data -- hard edged clinical data, in this

00:05:09.849 --> 00:05:14.189
case -- and actually has not done very much
at all in terms of understanding how to think

00:05:14.189 --> 00:05:18.659
about 'softer' intelligence; what other industries
would call 'market research' or 'customer

00:05:18.659 --> 00:05:21.199
insight' information.

00:05:21.199 --> 00:05:27.599
In a sense, they are different classes of
data: hard data and statistics we understand,

00:05:27.599 --> 00:05:29.789
but this softer intelligence is much more
fine.

00:05:29.789 --> 00:05:35.029
It is 'line of sight' insight into whether
things are going well or badly over time and

00:05:35.029 --> 00:05:38.860
looking at providers with a broader, more
generalised brush.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:43.650
We did not specify, for example, in the first
situation of friends and family test that

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.009
hospitals should be told how they were going
to collect it.

00:05:46.009 --> 00:05:49.020
We specified, obviously, the question that
they would be asking, but not how they would

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.229
collect it.

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:54.379
Actually, it does not really matter, because
what matters is the volume of people giving

00:05:54.379 --> 00:05:56.180
their responses to particular question.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:00.680
I wanted to make that point: that there is
a proper and robust debate going on about

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.830
whether or not we should refine the methodology
in this way or that way and obviously, we

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:08.949
are reflecting on it; but the overall importance
of this is a line of sight, softer measure

00:06:08.949 --> 00:06:11.460
of the way people are experiencing services.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:12.460
Care Connect
Overview

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:15.990
There are two other things I would like quickly
to highlight.

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:19.349
Alongside that, we have also launched another
initiative -- which at the moment certainly

00:06:19.349 --> 00:06:24.779
is not mandatory and is in a somewhat experimental
phase -- which is to try to use the power

00:06:24.779 --> 00:06:30.469
of social media and other channels to offer
people the opportunity to feed back directly

00:06:30.469 --> 00:06:36.589
to their clinician at their own convenience;
for example, 'I have been waiting too long

00:06:36.589 --> 00:06:41.180
in a ward', here, or, '...in a waiting room',
there, '...and I want to let my clinician

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:42.949
and my local hospital manager know that.'

00:06:42.949 --> 00:06:47.199
We have launched this programme, called 'Care
Connect' in London and the North East, which

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:52.999
is providing people with the opportunity to
text, tweet, ring in on a telephone number

00:06:52.999 --> 00:06:59.919
or email comments, complaints and compliments;
and those comments are then being reported

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:05.979
-- where the commenter is happy with us doing
so -- in real time on a map, so everybody

00:07:05.979 --> 00:07:06.979
can see it.

00:07:06.979 --> 00:07:11.039
This is the power of transparency and it is
driving awareness of complaints and comments

00:07:11.039 --> 00:07:12.289
in local hospitals.

00:07:12.289 --> 00:07:16.289
Reception
Already, again, that has proved to be welcomed

00:07:16.289 --> 00:07:18.069
by the hospitals that are participating in
the trial.

00:07:18.069 --> 00:07:20.610
They are finding it really useful, if quite
challenging.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:26.710
As you will note, there are one or two examples
here of people making, I think, important

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:31.039
comments about the way services are not delivering
as you would expect; but this is all part

00:07:31.039 --> 00:07:33.719
of the conversation which we need to develop.

00:07:33.719 --> 00:07:38.639
At the moment, we are looking at potentially
rolling out Care Connect as a national offer

00:07:38.639 --> 00:07:39.669
in the course of next year.

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:44.800
It is another thing, I hope, demonstrates
to everybody that NHS England is really very,

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.339
very serious about putting participation absolutely
at the heart of its agenda and priorities.

00:07:49.339 --> 00:07:53.379
Patient self reporting initiative
Finally, there is another initiative, which

00:07:53.379 --> 00:07:59.349
is also unprecedented and is, again, a demonstration
of our commitment to transparency and participation,

00:07:59.349 --> 00:08:10.530
which is the funding of 20 pilots within our
specialist commissioning area to give patients

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:13.499
the opportunity to start recording their own
data.

00:08:13.499 --> 00:08:18.499
A really important ingredient of being able
to commission for outcomes is that we understand

00:08:18.499 --> 00:08:22.020
that sometimes outcomes that a clinician might
value are not necessarily the same as those

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:26.409
which a patient might value; and we need to
have both bits of intelligence on the table

00:08:26.409 --> 00:08:31.740
when the patient and the doctor or the clinician
are having their discussions.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:36.849
This is about providing a very different route
to giving patients the opportunity to keep

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:41.750
their own record and for that to be given
a parity of esteem, in a way, with the clinical

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:43.490
data that is held over here.

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:47.240
You can understand, perhaps, the importance
of that in, say, cleft lip and palette services

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:52.530
where, for the parent and for the child, the
important outcome is speech development; the

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:56.180
surgeon, of course, will be worried about
harm in the theatre as the cleft is being

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.180
fixed.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:01.060
Neither of these things are unimportant: both
probably have equal importance, but they both

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:06.240
need to be present when we are thinking about
the kinds of outcomes we want to commission.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:10.120
That is a very exciting new initiative which
will be launched shortly, as well.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.150
Q&amp;A
Obviously, there are other things in the document.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:16.030
I am very happy to take any questions on those
things.

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:17.370
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Tim.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:20.930
I know we are going to come back to FFT when
we look at the performance report overall

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:26.860
and we can start to track trends, but let
me invite questions or comments from Members

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:28.660
of the Board.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:31.060
Victor Adebowale: Very good.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:32.060
This is excellent.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:40.650
I just wanted to invite Tim to emphasise that
this work is not just about hospitals; and

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:47.960
secondly, I am curious as to when we will
get a sense of how this feedback breaks down

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:57.160
by demographic, with reference to the ideas
of disadvantage.

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:59.440
When are we going to get a look at that?

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:04.200
Also, what kind of good practice is resulting
already as a consequence of this information

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:06.310
being fed back to providers?

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:12.280
Tim Kelsey: May I just say that of course
-- and sorry, I should have said -- it is

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:13.280
not just about hospitals.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:14.410
That is where we started.

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:19.900
To be clear, we are already committed to the
roll out of the friends and family test to

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:26.770
maternity departments in October -- that is
going well -- to all primary care contexts

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:32.690
by December of 2014 and to the entire NHS
by March of 2015.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:35.450
That is a public commitment, which we are
on track to deliver.

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:40.350
Absolutely, therefore, it is not just about
hospitals.

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:44.290
We had a very important conversation yesterday
about inequalities and the correct priority

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:48.120
we should be placing on demonstrated that
we are having impact there; and an absolutely

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:52.480
critical part of the way we use this feedback
to help inform us is going to be how it can

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:56.090
drive social justice in delivery of NHS services.

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:58.931
The friends and family test, at the moment,
does not properly capture that.

00:10:58.931 --> 00:11:02.920
That is one of the design refinements we are
going to be making, so that people can give

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:06.670
us some insight, when they are describing
whether they are satisfied with the local

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:10.841
service, into what their ethnic background
is and so on.

00:11:10.841 --> 00:11:12.530
That is something we will be adding.

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:15.060
Care Connect can already do that.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:20.010
Certainly, these sources of intelligence should
be very, very important to the way in which

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:24.510
we design our inequalities and equalities
strategy moving forward.

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:29.990
I am very mindful of that, but thank you for
the reminder.

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:31.480
Malcolm Grant: I think I have Bill McCarthy,
David, Moira and Naguib.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:32.480
Bill McCarthy: Thank you, Chairman.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:38.760
I wanted to pick up on Victor's first question,
'not just hospitals' and particularly to mention

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:39.870
mental health services.

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:48.930
Over the past few weeks, I have been speaking
both with chief executives and staff of mental

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:55.780
health care providers, as well as with a number
of service users and there is a great deal

00:11:55.780 --> 00:12:00.990
of enthusiasm in those services to embrace
the friends and family test.

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:11.090
Indeed, they start from quite a strong tradition
of really good, articulate engagement and

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:18.940
even though, as Tim said, the commitment is
not until 2015, there are certainly a whole

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:25.090
number of mental health providers who are
keen to get going early and certainly, we

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:30.040
are very happy to support that, as an important
part of demonstrating our commitment to parity

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:31.040
of esteem.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:32.380
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:33.380
David?

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:39.900
David Nicholson: I was at a mental health
provider last week and they absolutely want

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:40.930
to get into this.

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:45.490
In fact -- I have to say -- I was in Ashworth
Hospital and a patient at Ashworth Hospital

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:51.770
asked me why they did not get the opportunity
to fill in friends and family.

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:55.520
There is, therefore, a huge amount of enthusiasm
for it and I think we should go with that;

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:58.350
I think that is a really important part of
it.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:02.900
There is one thing which strikes me, though,
Tim.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:06.390
When we did 'High Quality Care for All', one
of the things we did was about what made a

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:11.860
great clinical team and what made a great
clinical team was people who measured things,

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:16.690
who set themselves goals and then delivered
those goals; and then started to think, 'How

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:24.170
am I doing compared with my colleagues in
other hospitals, etc.?' and they began to

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:25.500
benchmark themselves.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:29.760
You can absolutely see now how the friends
and family test and the benchmarking bit of

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:32.400
that, for individuals, will be more and more
important.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:37.880
As such, it seems to me that in terms of the
rigour with which we expect people to measure

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:43.080
it and the methodology, that pressure to be
more standardised about it will, I think,

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:46.270
become greater as we go through that, as people
look outside of their organisations.

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:55.130
The other point is the Care Connect stuff,
which I think is really, genuinely, for people

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:57.040
who are in hospitals, transformational.

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:01.290
I have been around a long time and I have
done lots of jobs, but I was talking to a

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:06.080
chief executive of a hospital in London where
it completely transforms the nature of their

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:11.590
jobs and what they do, because they know on
an hourly or daily basis what their patients

00:14:11.590 --> 00:14:15.610
are saying and what the issues are in their
hospital; and it changes the things that you

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:17.960
do.

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:22.470
The power of this, I think, is really, really
significant and could make such a massive

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:26.500
difference to the way all of us think about
our jobs and the things we do, which is precisely

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:28.060
the point of it.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:29.610
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:30.610
Moira?

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:31.940
Moira Gibb: Thank you, Malcolm.

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:38.170
I have two things on this which were prompted
by last night's AGM.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:43.530
I think it is really, really good and really
encouraging and I agree with David about the

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:44.530
potential.

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:49.690
While we congratulate ourselves for having
had our first AGM, however, we had a member

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:54.241
of the audience who pointed out there are
lots of public opportunities which have actually

00:14:54.241 --> 00:14:59.510
been lost in the new system: PCTs are not
having meetings in public.

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:06.610
I think you commit, Malcolm, to reflect on
that or perhaps, David, you said that you

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:14.040
would reflect on that; and I think that patient
and public voice is wider than just the Commissioning

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:15.040
Board.

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:20.560
Secondly, the other thing which we committed
to was the 'You Said, We Did', reporting back

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:28.060
next year and actually, it is much easier
for providers to have that kind of responsiveness.

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:33.300
As such, do you think it will help us on the
'You Said, We Did'; or do we need to have

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:37.380
another process in the sense of being clear?

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:43.000
There are lots of things that are being influenced
in what NHS England does from the patient

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:48.570
voice but it is a matter of capturing it,
really, for that accountability, in a very

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:49.820
short period of time.

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:50.820
Tim Kelsey: Yes.

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:52.600
There are two parts to the answer to that
question.

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:57.790
The first one is that Victor, of course -- it
is not in his paper -- will be bringing back

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:02.779
to the next meeting an update on the leading
work on designing the Citizens' Assembly,

00:16:02.779 --> 00:16:09.580
which will not just be a national, monolithic
thing; it will be all over the NHS, as it

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:14.250
were, as a social network and as a physical
set of conversations which are happening.

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:19.850
That has been through a series of very substantial
collaborative conversation with all sorts

00:16:19.850 --> 00:16:22.020
of different stakeholders and I think we are
getting to a pretty good place.

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:27.380
That may help with the specifics about how
NHS England can be held to account locally,

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:30.420
in lots of different care contexts, in a way
that is meaningful.

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:35.830
As you say, things perhaps have been lost
from the old system, which had different organisations

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:37.240
and so on.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:42.960
In terms of how we can demonstrate our own
-- I hope -- achievement and the degree to

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:47.800
which we have delivered on this commitment
to transparency, participation and to representing

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:51.321
the voices properly, I think the Patient Insight
Dashboard -- which is referenced in here -- is

00:16:51.321 --> 00:16:54.710
going to be a pretty important piece of that
conversation.

00:16:54.710 --> 00:17:00.890
As you will remember, we have committed to
creating an on line tool, which will be open

00:17:00.890 --> 00:17:05.151
to everybody, which has a variety of different
informational feeds on it which will, in a

00:17:05.151 --> 00:17:10.059
way, provide a point of aggregating things
like comments, complaints and general feedback

00:17:10.059 --> 00:17:14.480
in ways which will tell us whether we are
having any impact or not through the various

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:16.509
policies we are enacting.

00:17:16.509 --> 00:17:22.170
That will become a routine part of the Board's
work in a couple of months' time.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:26.620
It is currently going out to public consultation
and we are having a very big conversation

00:17:26.620 --> 00:17:29.369
-- as you can imagine -- with all sorts of
people about what that should look like and

00:17:29.369 --> 00:17:31.360
what kinds of data should be in it.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:33.929
I think that will be an important part of
answering the question, 'Have we done anything?'

00:17:33.929 --> 00:17:34.929
Naguib Kheraj: Yes.

00:17:34.929 --> 00:17:35.929
I agree with all the points made already.

00:17:35.929 --> 00:17:47.200
I have two questions to help understand how
we are going about doing this.

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:55.321
The data is being sourced at the operational
point of delivery by providers; can you help

00:17:55.321 --> 00:17:59.410
me understand who organises the collection
of the data?

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:05.279
Is it the hospitals, the staff themselves
or is your team out there?

00:18:05.279 --> 00:18:07.350
How does that work?

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:12.710
Can you also give us a sense of what we are
spending on this; and is the spending by NHS

00:18:12.710 --> 00:18:16.440
England or is it in the hospitals?

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:22.029
Tim Kelsey: The hospital collects the data.

00:18:22.029 --> 00:18:27.259
They manage the entire collection process
and they also manage the process of reporting

00:18:27.259 --> 00:18:29.190
the results of that data.

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:35.690
The data is considered an official statistic,
which imposes certain legal obligations on

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:42.652
the provider, for example, to collect it honestly
and without any sort of gaming -- to do it

00:18:42.652 --> 00:18:47.399
properly -- and actually, it is a criminal
offence to game this data and its collection,

00:18:47.399 --> 00:18:50.220
as with other official statistics.

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:51.600
That is their responsibility.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:57.809
The providers are incentivised through sequin
payments for meeting response rates that we

00:18:57.809 --> 00:19:04.711
think are satisfactory; 15% as a combined
response rate is the level you have to have

00:19:04.711 --> 00:19:08.549
reached in order to qualify for your sequin
incentive payment as a hospital.

00:19:08.549 --> 00:19:15.105
Naguib Kheraj: NHS makes a payment to the
hospital for doing that, then?

00:19:15.105 --> 00:19:18.520
Tim Kelsey: Well, the hospital is paid more
from its commissioner, whether that is NHS

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:23.970
England or the CCG, on an incentive basis
through that sequin mechanism.

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:33.429
The system is providing an incentive to the
hospital through the contract.

00:19:33.429 --> 00:19:37.220
The cost is very slight.

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:41.749
Many hospitals are running their collections
using online tools that are provided to them

00:19:41.749 --> 00:19:47.389
by third party commercial vendors for free,
so all they have to do is direct patients

00:19:47.389 --> 00:19:51.020
to the online form and invite them to fill
it in.

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:56.289
The rest of the processing is managed by organisations
who are actually providing that service for

00:19:56.289 --> 00:19:57.410
free at the moment.

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:59.509
The cost overall is very slight.

00:19:59.509 --> 00:20:05.679
Clearly, NHS England has invested its funds,
which are pretty limited, just in providing

00:20:05.679 --> 00:20:12.269
guidance and support; and we are on the ground
helping hospitals which are finding it difficult

00:20:12.269 --> 00:20:15.020
to manage response rates, with the best support
we can.

00:20:15.020 --> 00:20:18.980
As such, there is an element of being on the
ground, but we are not responsible for the

00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:20.279
collection itself.

00:20:20.279 --> 00:20:24.309
Naguib Kheraj: Some of the coverage in the
media this summer about the costs is completely

00:20:24.309 --> 00:20:25.309
off.

00:20:25.309 --> 00:20:26.309
Tim Kelsey: Yes, completely.

00:20:26.309 --> 00:20:29.669
Just to be clear, there was coverage in the
media about staggeringly large costs associated

00:20:29.669 --> 00:20:34.139
with the friends and family test and I still
cannot quite find out where that had come

00:20:34.139 --> 00:20:39.460
from, but I think that reflects original impact
assessments which were done some time ago

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:44.080
by the Department of Health, based on assumptions
I cannot speak for.

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:47.710
As things stand, the costs are very low.

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:56.460
Jane Cummings: I wanted to respond to Moira's
question a little more, which was, 'What have

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:57.789
we done as a result?'

00:20:57.789 --> 00:21:02.530
I thought it was worth updating the Board
on a further pilot that is going to be done

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:08.269
in the North of England with 35 organisations,
which is linked to the transparency work and

00:21:08.269 --> 00:21:11.470
also to Compassion in Practice, the nursing
strategy.

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:18.230
What they are planning to do is collect information
and publish around incidence of falls and

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:25.980
pressure ulcers, for example, but to link
that with FFT and staff experience responses

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:29.169
and to publish that on a monthly basis.

00:21:29.169 --> 00:21:33.059
The key part about this is that every month
there will be a, 'You Said, We Did'.

00:21:33.059 --> 00:21:38.809
There will be a description of what they have
done at a very granular level -- for example,

00:21:38.809 --> 00:21:43.619
a ward or service level -- which describes
how they have responded to both incidents

00:21:43.619 --> 00:21:48.850
of potential or actual harm -- to patients
who have fallen, for example -- but also what

00:21:48.850 --> 00:21:53.730
staff have said about their experience and
what the patients have said about their experiences.

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:56.940
I think the first publication of that is likely
to be towards the end of November.

00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:02.259
That will be 35 organisations across the board
looking at what they are doing and being very

00:22:02.259 --> 00:22:05.730
open and transparent about what they have
actually done as a result, which I think will

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:07.039
be really meaningful to people.

00:22:07.039 --> 00:22:08.379
Malcolm Grant: Yes.

00:22:08.379 --> 00:22:14.269
Ed Smith: Can I just absolutely reinforce
what David said about Care Connect?

00:22:14.269 --> 00:22:19.869
That, to me, is a fantastic initiative and
I think it would be great for the Board to

00:22:19.869 --> 00:22:21.600
see how that develops.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:24.379
I think it is -- as David said -- transformation
on the ground.

00:22:24.379 --> 00:22:30.059
It would be helpful to have a view as to whether
you have shown it to Julie Mellor yet and

00:22:30.059 --> 00:22:36.249
whether the Health Service Ombudsman sees
that as an advance on, shall we say, the messaging

00:22:36.249 --> 00:22:38.159
that she was getting back about complaints.

00:22:38.159 --> 00:22:43.409
Tim Kelsey: Care Connect, to be clear, was
intended originally just as an experiment

00:22:43.409 --> 00:22:48.420
for seeing the degree to which there is a
public appetite for, in a way, sharing the

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:55.739
job of managing hospitals or other provider
institutions.

00:22:55.739 --> 00:23:03.119
It does, however, potentially have real value,
I think, in being the vehicle through which

00:23:03.119 --> 00:23:09.149
the health and care system as a whole can
offer a much easier point of access to people

00:23:09.149 --> 00:23:11.369
who want to make complaints.

00:23:11.369 --> 00:23:14.239
I have spoken to Julie about it.

00:23:14.239 --> 00:23:18.482
She, I think, is very enthusiastic about the
opportunities for working with us -- and indeed,

00:23:18.482 --> 00:23:23.880
other regulators, as well -- on developing
Care Connect as a mechanism not just for feedback,

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:29.809
but also to help direct people in a much more
accessible, easy fashion, to different points

00:23:29.809 --> 00:23:32.809
in the system where they might want to make
a complaint about a particular service.

00:23:32.809 --> 00:23:39.289
We are consequently working with her very
closely at the moment and when and if we roll

00:23:39.289 --> 00:23:44.429
this out nationally, that will be, I think,
a very core part of the offer we are making

00:23:44.429 --> 00:23:45.429
to patients.

00:23:45.429 --> 00:23:46.429
Malcolm Grant: Good.

00:23:46.429 --> 00:23:47.429
Thank you very much, Tim, for the paper and
for the report.

00:23:47.429 --> 00:23:51.830
I think this is one of the most innovative
things that we are doing and I know the Board

00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:54.330
would wish to commend the work of you are
your team.

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:59.309
We need to remind ourselves that this is one
of the five domains that we are required to

00:23:59.309 --> 00:24:06.619
report against, to ensure that we understand
what the satisfaction of patients and the

00:24:06.619 --> 00:24:09.641
public is with what we provide by way of the
NHS.

00:24:09.641 --> 00:24:14.409
Secondly, our responsibility is to commission
the highest quality healthcare and we need

00:24:14.409 --> 00:24:18.850
to find out what the consumers of high quality
healthcare actually think about it; and I

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:26.429
think the FFT and the care data material is
absolutely critical to our understanding of

00:24:26.429 --> 00:24:27.429
that.

00:24:27.429 --> 00:24:32.929
It is early stages and this is highly experimental
but I think already we have a feeling of some

00:24:32.929 --> 00:24:34.879
assurance to the Board that we are going in
the right direction.

00:24:34.879 --> 00:24:39.929
I think the third element of it -- which you
emphasised in your remarks -- is how it drives

00:24:39.929 --> 00:24:42.330
improvement across the system.

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:49.580
It opens up an understanding of patient satisfaction
with services and drives a positive response

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:54.700
which otherwise does not get done, because
people feel very frustrated about not having

00:24:54.700 --> 00:24:58.929
a mechanism to express their concerns about
the service they are getting.

00:24:58.929 --> 00:25:04.899
In one sense, it is simple consumerism; but
in another sense, I think it is a dynamic

00:25:04.899 --> 00:25:08.239
force for change throughout the NHS.

00:25:08.239 --> 00:25:13.919
It will come back to us this morning in the
performance report and we will, at every meeting,

00:25:13.919 --> 00:25:17.769
be tracking our performance through that mechanism.

00:25:17.769 --> 00:25:22.869
I think the salience of the issue is demonstrated
by the positioning of the paper this morning

00:25:22.869 --> 00:25:28.070
at the head of the agenda for the Board meeting
and the attention which we have given to it.

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:29.409
Thank you very much for that.

00:25:29.409 --> 00:25:33.730
The next item on the agenda, Tim, I am afraid,
is back to you, which is the publication of

00:25:33.730 --> 00:25:35.295
the directions to the Health &amp; Social Care
Information Centre.

00:25:35.295 --> 00:25:37.289
Publication of Directions to the Health and
Social Care Information Centre

00:25:37.289 --> 00:25:38.970
Tim Kelsey
National Director for Patients and Information

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:43.029
The value of data
In a different but related way, this is just

00:25:43.029 --> 00:25:45.080
as important as all the things we have also
talked about.

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:51.009
One of the biggest challenges, not just to
commissioning for outcomes but for improving

00:25:51.009 --> 00:25:55.529
outcomes in healthcare, is the absence in
large parts of data with which we can actually

00:25:55.529 --> 00:25:57.480
measure the quality of healthcare.

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:02.549
There are still very, very many fundamental
questions which commissioners and patients

00:26:02.549 --> 00:26:06.550
will want to ask, which simple cannot be answered
without better access to data.

00:26:06.550 --> 00:26:15.619
I have presented to the Board in the past
on Care.data, which is our programme to transform

00:26:15.619 --> 00:26:16.779
data services in healthcare.

00:26:16.779 --> 00:26:21.749
We are working very closely with our partner,
the Health and Social Care Information Centre.

00:26:21.749 --> 00:26:27.919
The first phase of this, I think, frankly
radical, transformative programme to put better

00:26:27.919 --> 00:26:33.590
data in the hands of clinicians and patients
is to provide them with, for the first time,

00:26:33.590 --> 00:26:39.120
proper routine access to anonymised information
about primary care services and in particular,

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:40.470
general practice.

00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:46.179
We have spent a lot of time consulting on
the nature of that request and recognise absolutely

00:26:46.179 --> 00:26:54.200
the proper concerns that both primary care
clinicians and the public at large have around

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:59.679
confidentiality, to make sure that we balance
any requirements for data with very clear

00:26:59.679 --> 00:27:06.499
evidence of benefit in its extraction and
at the same time guarantee confidentiality

00:27:06.499 --> 00:27:07.759
to patients.

00:27:07.759 --> 00:27:11.549
Collaboration
The paper before you describes the specifics

00:27:11.549 --> 00:27:15.299
in relation to the extraction of data from
general practice.

00:27:15.299 --> 00:27:20.059
We are required legally to direct the Health
and Social Care Information Centre to begin

00:27:20.059 --> 00:27:21.109
the extraction.

00:27:21.109 --> 00:27:27.470
The extraction has been agreed and I would
also like to thank them for their support

00:27:27.470 --> 00:27:30.479
by the BMA and the Royal College of General
Practice.

00:27:30.479 --> 00:27:36.059
We are working very closely with the Information
Commissioner on how best to promote this new

00:27:36.059 --> 00:27:40.190
initiative to the public so that they are
fully aware of their rights in relation to

00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:42.320
the data that is being extracted.

00:27:42.320 --> 00:27:46.960
There has been a lot of misreporting about
this in the media and there have been quite

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:52.580
a few corrections published in the same media
following subsequent conversations.

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:56.259
Confidentiality
Essentially, the important features of this

00:27:56.259 --> 00:28:02.659
are that it is about anonymised data being
shared with third parties who are commissioning

00:28:02.659 --> 00:28:09.370
services, progressing science and developing
new and innovative approaches to patient care.

00:28:09.370 --> 00:28:15.559
No confidential data is going to be circulated
to those parties without specific Parliamentary

00:28:15.559 --> 00:28:23.299
approvals, so headlines saying that confidential
data is being sold for £1 are completely

00:28:23.299 --> 00:28:27.429
erroneous, factually utterly incorrect and
have largely been corrected.

00:28:27.429 --> 00:28:33.369
The 'sold for £1' part reflects our other
commitment, which is safely to make this data

00:28:33.369 --> 00:28:36.179
as widely available as we can.

00:28:36.179 --> 00:28:40.470
We want innovators of all sorts to be able
to access it, so we are not going to charge

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:41.470
them for it.

00:28:41.470 --> 00:28:45.990
'For £1' means, effectively, that we are
giving it away for free, because we believe

00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:50.140
that the greatest value to the taxpayer and
British economy is going to be in the sorts

00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:57.080
of enterprises that will bring development
in life sciences and elsewhere by giving people

00:28:57.080 --> 00:29:01.389
much freer access to this amazingly valuable
data.

00:29:01.389 --> 00:29:04.440
Commendation
The first part of the programme is better

00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:08.190
to inform commissioning; that is what the
instructions and the Information Centre is

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:09.190
for.

00:29:09.190 --> 00:29:13.129
I would commend it to the Board and hope that
the Board will share with me a real sense

00:29:13.129 --> 00:29:18.739
of this being a genuine turning point in the
development of our Health Service.

00:29:18.739 --> 00:29:23.019
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:29:23.019 --> 00:29:26.299
Are there any questions for Tim?

00:29:26.299 --> 00:29:30.600
I think the legal process here is a very straightforward
one.

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:35.960
What it unlocks, of course, is the great wealth
of data that the NHS has always had but which

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:41.869
has been, let us say, less than totally effective
in deploying to the benefit not only of commissioning

00:29:41.869 --> 00:29:46.259
but also, more generally, of understanding
which interventions work and understanding

00:29:46.259 --> 00:29:47.259
the system as a whole.

00:29:47.259 --> 00:29:59.651
Ciarán Devane: I have a comment from, I think,
a patient point of view: the vast majority

00:29:59.651 --> 00:30:01.070
of patients have an expectation that the data
will be shared for the greater good, so that

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:04.729
the next person to have the same condition
will have a better chance of recovery.

00:30:04.729 --> 00:30:10.870
As such, I think there is a real momentum
behind this, subject to all the usual safeguards.

00:30:10.870 --> 00:30:14.049
I think this is the beginning of something
which will be really powerful and I think

00:30:14.049 --> 00:30:17.639
there is a lot of support out there for it,
so well done.

00:30:17.639 --> 00:30:22.239
Bruce Keogh: Mine is a follow on from Ciarán's,
really.

00:30:22.239 --> 00:30:31.179
People are very supportive of this, subject
to concerns about confidentiality, Tim, particularly

00:30:31.179 --> 00:30:36.330
in the face of some of the stuff which has
been in the media over the last year or so.

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:40.070
I wonder if you could spell out a little bit
more about the safeguards.

00:30:40.070 --> 00:30:44.379
Tim Kelsey: Yes and thank you for asking that
question.

00:30:44.379 --> 00:30:50.529
I wanted to be really clear that the overriding
priority in this programme and in all the

00:30:50.529 --> 00:30:55.269
other work we are doing to liberate data,
as it were, within the Health Service, is

00:30:55.269 --> 00:30:56.940
patient confidentiality.

00:30:56.940 --> 00:31:01.080
We have taken the rather exceptional step
-- which has been previously noted by the

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:09.320
Board -- to encourage and -- as we must -- advertise
to people, patients and the public at large

00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:15.549
their legal rights in relation to this data,
which are that they can object to its extraction

00:31:15.549 --> 00:31:17.289
from their general practice.

00:31:17.289 --> 00:31:23.220
Currently, the law would then require the
GP to make a judgment about whether or not

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:26.979
that objection, in their view, is reasonable;
they are the data controller and that is the

00:31:26.979 --> 00:31:27.979
law.

00:31:27.979 --> 00:31:31.630
We have gone one step further with this release
to say that when somebody objects to that

00:31:31.630 --> 00:31:35.830
extraction, for whatever reason -- even though
we all think it is the right thing to do,

00:31:35.830 --> 00:31:39.580
people may choose to take a different view
-- that objection, regardless of what the

00:31:39.580 --> 00:31:42.289
GP thinks, will be upheld.

00:31:42.289 --> 00:31:46.830
In a de facto sense, not only are we guaranteeing
people's confidentiality, but we are actually

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:52.739
raising the bar on giving them proper choice
as to whether they want to participate.

00:31:52.739 --> 00:31:55.759
I hope that will set a standard not just for
healthcare, but across government.

00:31:55.759 --> 00:32:01.849
One thing I think we can do as NHS England
is to be just absolutely clear that this is

00:32:01.849 --> 00:32:03.529
an absolute priority for us.

00:32:03.529 --> 00:32:10.249
If we, for one second, lose public confidence
in the way in which we are conducting our

00:32:10.249 --> 00:32:13.230
commissioning analysis on their behalf, we
are sunk.

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:21.559
Bruce Keogh: Tim, when data extraction was
sued for Summary Care Record, it was the GPs

00:32:21.559 --> 00:32:27.250
themselves who were concerned about the level
of confidentiality and legitimately so.

00:32:27.250 --> 00:32:33.259
I wonder how well you have engaged them, so
that they become advocates.

00:32:33.259 --> 00:32:34.649
Tim Kelsey: Yes.

00:32:34.649 --> 00:32:38.899
As I said, we spent a lot of time with the
British Medical Association and the Royal

00:32:38.899 --> 00:32:42.360
College of General Practice, where there was
a lot of properly robust discussion around

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:50.440
the degree to which we were going properly
to safeguard patients' confidentiality.

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:57.940
There is a quite intensive, wide, large scale
programme to make sure that every GP in the

00:32:57.940 --> 00:33:03.059
country is absolutely clear about what this
is about, is clear about their obligations

00:33:03.059 --> 00:33:09.869
in relation to advertising to their own patients
their rights and so on; and that is a combination

00:33:09.869 --> 00:33:15.529
of direct mail and meetings and conferences.

00:33:15.529 --> 00:33:21.000
We have invested, really, quite a lot of time
and resource in making sure that we do get

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:25.970
round and explain to every GP and hopefully
we are able to deal with their issues.

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:30.470
That is not to say, though, that there is
not a minority of GPs who have expressed directly

00:33:30.470 --> 00:33:33.599
to me real concerns about this.

00:33:33.599 --> 00:33:42.729
My belief is that, first of all, we will be
able to demonstrate quickly that we as a system

00:33:42.729 --> 00:33:48.239
are absolutely more than capable -- as we
did with the Summary Care Record -- of properly

00:33:48.239 --> 00:33:52.119
safeguarding people's confidentiality; and
perhaps more importantly, quickly demonstrated

00:33:52.119 --> 00:33:56.450
what benefit this data can bring to patient
outcomes.

00:33:56.450 --> 00:34:02.279
A combination of those things, I hope, will
allay the concerns even of the most worried

00:34:02.279 --> 00:34:03.279
general practitioner.

00:34:03.279 --> 00:34:08.840
The vast majority, however, from our work
so far, are supportive and actually quite

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:14.330
genuinely pleased with the way in which we
have addressed this issue of automatically

00:34:14.330 --> 00:34:17.330
upholding people's objections where they are
made.

00:34:17.330 --> 00:34:21.610
Naguib Kheraj: My points were building on
the same issue, really.

00:34:21.610 --> 00:34:29.980
For clarification, is it the GPs themselves
who are responsible for making sure that the

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:32.780
data is anonymised, so that that happens at
the GP practice?

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:33.960
Is that right?

00:34:33.960 --> 00:34:35.610
Tim Kelsey: No.

00:34:35.610 --> 00:34:40.190
One of the most important values that this
initiative brings is the ability for this

00:34:40.190 --> 00:34:43.270
data -- the GP data -- to be linked to hospital
data.

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:48.159
At the moment, we have no means of assessing
an outcome across the pathway of a patient's

00:34:48.159 --> 00:34:52.780
care, except in very specific, audited circumstances.

00:34:52.780 --> 00:34:59.060
To do that, the data needs to be extracted
into the safe haven of the Information Centre

00:34:59.060 --> 00:35:02.960
-- which is the legal safe haven, under the
Health and Social Care Act, which is allowed

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:07.560
to handle patient identifiable data -- so
that it can be linked to hospital data and

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:08.560
then anonymised.

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:12.130
Consequently, the anonymisation takes place
in the Information Centre, not in the GP practice.

00:35:12.130 --> 00:35:14.980
Naguib Kheraj: Who has the managerial responsibility
for that?

00:35:14.980 --> 00:35:19.940
Tim Kelsey: The Information Centre is an independent,
arms length body with its own board and in

00:35:19.940 --> 00:35:24.130
fact, yesterday published its code of practice,
which reinforces all the things I have said

00:35:24.130 --> 00:35:28.500
about patient confidentiality and so on.

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:33.230
The purpose of this paper is that we, as an
AL -- arms length -- body, are required legally,

00:35:33.230 --> 00:35:40.240
in implementation of our own legal responsibilities
from the Act, to instruct them, as an independent

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:41.410
body, to go ahead and make the collection.

00:35:41.410 --> 00:35:46.650
It is their responsibility, however, safely
to handle the linkage of the identifiable

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:48.120
data within their own hands.

00:35:48.120 --> 00:35:54.470
Naguib Kheraj: Who has any kinds of rights
to audit that this is going on properly?

00:35:54.470 --> 00:36:01.200
Would we be involved in that or will the Information
Centre be responsible for that, too?

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:07.410
Tim Kelsey: There are quite a number of bodies
which have an interest in this, from a number

00:36:07.410 --> 00:36:08.410
of different directions.

00:36:08.410 --> 00:36:12.620
For example, the Care Quality Commission is
interested in data quality and will be, as

00:36:12.620 --> 00:36:16.910
part of its routine inspections, looking at
the quality of data that is generated across

00:36:16.910 --> 00:36:18.550
care contexts.

00:36:18.550 --> 00:36:24.930
The Information Commissioner, of course, and
has an interest in understanding how providers

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:28.590
in the NHS are managing their obligations
under the Data Protection Act.

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:33.890
We will have a big interest in auditing the
degree to which benefit is being derived from

00:36:33.890 --> 00:36:40.510
this data in the Service, so I think our interests
will be understanding and monitoring the degree

00:36:40.510 --> 00:36:44.350
to which people are using the data, particularly
to commission better for patient outcomes

00:36:44.350 --> 00:36:45.350
and so on and so forth.

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:48.430
As such, I think more or less everybody has
an interest in one way or another.

00:36:48.430 --> 00:36:52.220
It is a very good point you make, though,
about the degree to which we can rationalise

00:36:52.220 --> 00:36:56.710
all those requirements into an audit which
would minimise any burden on providers.

00:36:56.710 --> 00:36:59.730
I might take that away and have a think about
it.

00:36:59.730 --> 00:37:01.810
Malcolm Grant: Okay, Tim.

00:37:01.810 --> 00:37:02.850
Thank you.

00:37:02.850 --> 00:37:07.150
We have the ability to make this the very
best in the world because of the integrated

00:37:07.150 --> 00:37:08.390
nature of the NHS.

00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:13.710
What are the risks and benefits that we can
see from other jurisdictions who have attempted

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:17.200
to do this sort of work?

00:37:17.200 --> 00:37:22.830
Tim Kelsey: In a way, the best healthcare
systems in the world, none of them national,

00:37:22.830 --> 00:37:31.250
are those that have just prioritised the safe
sharing of data and alongside that, the proper

00:37:31.250 --> 00:37:33.710
exploitation of the values that technology
can bring.

00:37:33.710 --> 00:37:38.550
These two are indivisible: without data, we
cannot properly exploit the opportunity that

00:37:38.550 --> 00:37:40.100
technology offers us.

00:37:40.100 --> 00:37:42.160
They are also the best health systems in the
world.

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:50.010
To give you one example, the Veterans Administration,
which is a health system which serves the

00:37:50.010 --> 00:37:56.350
armed forces in the US, is completely automated
in ways we can only really aspire to at this

00:37:56.350 --> 00:38:07.370
point and has developed all sorts of best
in class data sharing programmes.

00:38:07.370 --> 00:38:11.210
They have also subjected themselves to all
sorts of peer reviewed examination.

00:38:11.210 --> 00:38:16.920
They say that a combination of proper use
of data with proper exploitation of the technology

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:23.750
that is then enabled has cut their bed day
admissions, for example, by 25%.

00:38:23.750 --> 00:38:27.510
They are a world class quality provider of
healthcare.

00:38:27.510 --> 00:38:29.620
There are many other examples.

00:38:29.620 --> 00:38:38.870
None of them have our complexity or our scale,
but they offer us a good evidence base of

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:44.110
why this is so important and perhaps fundamental
to the delivery of high quality healthcare

00:38:44.110 --> 00:38:45.110
for all.

00:38:45.110 --> 00:38:48.490
Malcolm Grant: Have they all been successful
in maintaining security and confidentiality?

00:38:48.490 --> 00:38:53.170
Tim Kelsey: I cannot answer that question
but to my knowledge, yes.

00:38:53.170 --> 00:38:55.750
I would have to go and have a look, however.

00:38:55.750 --> 00:38:56.920
Malcolm Grant: Okay.

00:38:56.920 --> 00:38:57.920
Thank you very much.

00:38:57.920 --> 00:38:59.560
I think we have had a really good review of
the programme.

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:00.560
May we, then, now move on to item six on the
agenda, which is implementing the recommendations

00:39:00.560 --> 00:39:00.567
of the Francis Report and Winterbourne View?

